```{r setup, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE,
  comment = "#>"
)
has_lavaan <- requireNamespace("lavaan", quietly = TRUE)
```

```{r}
library(DMAR)
```

Reporting the reliability of a composite score involves two separable
decisions, following the framework of Kelley and Pornprasertmanit
(2016): choosing the coefficient and choosing the interval.

* **Choosing the coefficient.** The coefficient reported is a claim
  about the measurement model and about which composite is scored.
  Each coefficient below estimates the proportion of composite
  variance attributable to the common factor, under different
  commitments about the model. Choosing a coefficient is choosing
  which commitments the analysis makes.
* **Choosing the interval.** Given the coefficient, the confidence
  interval method is an empirical performance question: which
  procedures cover the population value at their nominal rate across
  realistic conditions. The Monte Carlo studies of Kelley and
  Pornprasertmanit (2016) addressed that question, and the DMAR
  defaults encode their recommendations.

The map for choosing the coefficient:

| Function | The composite | The claim being made |
|---|---|---|
| `reliability_alpha()` | unit-weighted sum | one factor with *equal loadings* (essential tau-equivalence) |
| `reliability_kr20()` | sum of 0/1 items | alpha's claim, for binary items |
| `reliability_omega()` (robust omega, the default) | unit-weighted sum | one congeneric factor for the *numerator only*; the composite variance is estimated from the data |
| `reliability_omega(denominator = "model_implied")` | unit-weighted sum | one congeneric factor; the fitted model reproduces the composite variance |
| `reliability_omega_categorical()` | sum of observed ordinal categories | a probit threshold model links items to one factor for the *numerator only*; the total variance comes from the unrestricted polychoric correlations |
| `reliability_omega_categorical(denominator = "model_implied")` | sum of observed ordinal categories | a probit threshold model links items to one factor; the fitted model reproduces the polychoric correlations (Green & Yang, 2009) |
| `reliability_H()` | *optimally weighted* composite | one congeneric factor; a different composite, not a different assumption |

The vignette walks the table top to bottom on one running example,
then turns to the interval.

## A Running Example

A six-item congeneric scale with unequal loadings, which is the
normal state of real items:

```{r}
set.seed(113)
N <- 300
lambda <- c(0.4, 0.5, 0.6, 0.7, 0.75, 0.8)
eta <- rnorm(N)
items <- sweep(matrix(rep(eta, 6), N, 6), 2, lambda, `*`) +
  matrix(rnorm(N * 6), N, 6) %*% diag(sqrt(1 - lambda^2))
colnames(items) <- paste0("y", seq_len(6))
```

## Alpha Assumes Equal Loadings

Coefficient alpha equals the population reliability when the items
are essentially tau-equivalent, that is, when every item loads
equally on the common factor. When loadings differ, alpha
underestimates. On the running example the loadings range from .4 to
.8, so alpha sits a little below omega:

```{r alpha-omega, eval = has_lavaan}
res_alpha <- reliability_alpha(data = items, ci_method = "none")
res_omega <- reliability_omega(data = items,
                               denominator = "model_implied",
                               ci_method = "none")
c(alpha = res_alpha$value[res_alpha$term == "estimate"],
  omega = res_omega$value[res_omega$term == "estimate"])
```

The gap here is modest because the loadings, though unequal, are of
similar magnitude. The gap grows when the tau-equivalence assumption
fails badly, and with real tests a common way for it to fail is in how
the composite is scored rather than in the tests themselves. Four of
the tests in the Holzinger and Swineford (1939) data
(`holzinger_swineford`) measure spatial ability: visual perception,
cubes, paper form board, and lozenges. Because the tests are scored on
different scales, their raw-score loadings on a common factor are far
from equal, and the unit-weighted sum of the raw scores, the composite
alpha and omega describe here, carries that inequality. `cfa_1()` fits
the single factor with the loadings free, the congeneric model behind
omega, and with the loadings constrained to be equal, the essentially
tau-equivalent model behind alpha:

```{r spatial, eval = has_lavaan}
spatial <- holzinger_swineford[, c("t1_visual_perception", "t2_cubes",
                                   "t3_paper_form_board", "t4_lozenges")]
d_spatial <- descriptives(spatial)$descriptives
print(d_spatial[, c("variable", "mean", "sd")], digits = 3)
congeneric <- cfa_1(data = spatial)
congeneric[startsWith(congeneric$term, "lambda") |
             congeneric$term %in% c("chi_square", "df"),
           c("syntax", "term", "estimate")]
tau_equivalent <- cfa_1(data = spatial, equal_loading = TRUE)
tau_equivalent[tau_equivalent$term %in% c("chi_square", "df"),
               c("term", "estimate")]
a_spatial <- reliability_alpha(data = spatial, ci_method = "none")
o_spatial <- reliability_omega(data = spatial,
                               denominator = "model_implied",
                               ci_method = "none")
est_spatial <- c(alpha = a_spatial$value[a_spatial$term == "estimate"],
                 omega = o_spatial$value[o_spatial$term == "estimate"])
est_spatial
```

The standard deviations of the four tests run from
`r if (has_lavaan) formatC(min(d_spatial$sd), format = "f", digits = 2) else "2.83"`
(paper form board) to
`r if (has_lavaan) formatC(max(d_spatial$sd), format = "f", digits = 2) else "9.05"`
(lozenges), and the raw-score loadings follow them. The congeneric
model reproduces the covariances of the four tests closely, and
constraining the loadings to be equal does not. For the sum of the raw
scores, alpha falls
`r if (has_lavaan) formatC(unname(diff(est_spatial)), format = "f", digits = 3) else "0.057"`
below omega, against
`r if (has_lavaan) formatC(res_omega$value[res_omega$term == "estimate"] - res_alpha$value[res_alpha$term == "estimate"], format = "f", digits = 3) else "0.009"`
on the running example, so alpha here answers under an assumption this
composite violates.

Tau-equivalence is a property of the composite as scored, not of the
tests alone. Standardizing each test before summing gives every test
the same variance and makes a different composite, the sum of the
*z*-scores:

```{r spatial-standardized, eval = has_lavaan}
spatial_z <- as.data.frame(scale(spatial))
congeneric_z <- cfa_1(data = spatial_z)
congeneric_z[startsWith(congeneric_z$term, "lambda"),
             c("syntax", "term", "estimate")]
a_z <- reliability_alpha(data = spatial_z, ci_method = "none")
o_z <- reliability_omega(data = spatial_z,
                         denominator = "model_implied",
                         ci_method = "none")
est_z <- c(alpha = a_z$value[a_z$term == "estimate"],
           omega = o_z$value[o_z$term == "estimate"])
est_z
```

The standardized loadings run from
`r if (has_lavaan) sub("^0", "", formatC(min(congeneric_z$estimate[startsWith(congeneric_z$term, "lambda")]), format = "f", digits = 3)) else ".476"`
to
`r if (has_lavaan) sub("^0", "", formatC(max(congeneric_z$estimate[startsWith(congeneric_z$term, "lambda")]), format = "f", digits = 3)) else ".675"`,
a narrower spread than the running example's .4 to .8, and on this
composite alpha falls only
`r if (has_lavaan) formatC(unname(diff(est_z)), format = "f", digits = 3) else "0.005"`
below omega. The sum of the raw scores and the sum of the standardized
scores are different composites with different reliabilities, and a
reported coefficient is a claim about the composite actually scored.
The pattern generalizes: each coefficient is correct under its stated
model, and the choice among them is a choice among models.

## A Hypothetical Example: Items With Widely Unequal Loadings

On the standardized spatial tests the gap all but closed, because
their standardized loadings differ little. Items with equal variances
can still differ widely in how strongly they reflect the factor, and
then alpha falls well below omega. The items here are hypothetical,
constructed to show the point: six items, each with population
variance 1, three loading .9 on the factor and three loading .1, as in
a scale where half of the items barely track the construct. The
population alpha and omega follow from the loadings:

```{r hypothetical-population}
lambda_hyp <- c(0.9, 0.9, 0.9, 0.1, 0.1, 0.1)
k_hyp <- length(lambda_hyp)
Sigma_hyp <- tcrossprod(lambda_hyp) + diag(1 - lambda_hyp^2)
# Alpha from the item variances and the variance of the sum; omega as
# the share of the variance of the sum that the factor accounts for.
pop_hyp <- c(alpha = k_hyp / (k_hyp - 1) *
               (1 - sum(diag(Sigma_hyp)) / sum(Sigma_hyp)),
             omega = sum(lambda_hyp)^2 / sum(Sigma_hyp))
pop_hyp
```

Then 300 respondents are drawn from this population, and both
coefficients are estimated as before. The draws reuse the running
example's seed, so the factor scores and the normal deviates behind the
errors are those of the running example, and only the loadings differ:

```{r hypothetical-sample, eval = has_lavaan}
# withr::with_seed() restores the generator state afterward, so drawing
# these items does not change the random numbers the later examples use.
N_hyp <- 300
items_hyp <- withr::with_seed(113, {
  eta_hyp <- rnorm(N_hyp)
  sweep(matrix(rep(eta_hyp, k_hyp), N_hyp, k_hyp), 2, lambda_hyp, `*`) +
    matrix(rnorm(N_hyp * k_hyp), N_hyp, k_hyp) %*%
    diag(sqrt(1 - lambda_hyp^2))
})
colnames(items_hyp) <- paste0("y", seq_len(k_hyp))
a_hyp <- reliability_alpha(data = items_hyp, ci_method = "none")
o_hyp <- reliability_omega(data = items_hyp,
                           denominator = "model_implied",
                           ci_method = "none")
est_hyp <- c(alpha = a_hyp$value[a_hyp$term == "estimate"],
             omega = o_hyp$value[o_hyp$term == "estimate"])
est_hyp
```

In the population, alpha falls
`r formatC(unname(diff(pop_hyp)), format = "f", digits = 3)`
below omega, and in the sample,
`r if (has_lavaan) formatC(unname(diff(est_hyp)), format = "f", digits = 3) else "0.087"`,
against
`r if (has_lavaan) formatC(unname(diff(est_spatial)), format = "f", digits = 3) else "0.057"`
for the sum of the raw spatial scores and
`r if (has_lavaan) formatC(res_omega$value[res_omega$term == "estimate"] - res_alpha$value[res_alpha$term == "estimate"], format = "f", digits = 3) else "0.009"`
on the running example, whose six items also have variance 1. These
items already share one variance, so standardizing them leaves the gap
in place: alpha falls below omega because the equal loadings it
assumes are far from the loadings that generated the items.

## The Denominator Choice in Omega

Coefficient omega requires an estimate of the composite's total
variance in its denominator, and `reliability_omega()` offers two.
The default, `denominator = "observed"` (robust omega), estimates the
composite variance directly from the data, which is consistent for
the actual variance of the scale score whether or not the one-factor
model is right. The alternative, `denominator = "model_implied"`,
uses the total variance reproduced by the fitted single-factor model,
which is correct exactly when the one-factor model reproduces the
composite variance.

The coefficient was built for the following situation. Model misfit
is treated as a set of minor common factors, visible as residual
correlations: the scale is then not perfectly unidimensional, but a
single-factor approximation is retained. The coefficient isolates the
variance attributable to the general factor only, excluding the minor
factors, and expresses it relative to the observed variance of the
unweighted composite. Kelley and Pornprasertmanit (2016) named it
hierarchical omega after that hierarchical factor logic; upon
reflection, we would have named it observed omega or robust omega,
and DMAR uses *robust omega*. Two qualifications keep the name
accurate. The robustness is to misspecification of the total variance
only, because the numerator remains model based under either
denominator; and robust omega is distinct both from outlier-robust
estimation of alpha and omega (Zhang & Yuan, 2016) and from the
robust maximum-likelihood standard errors available through
`ci_method`.

When the one-factor model holds, the two agree, as on the running
example:

```{r denom-clean, eval = has_lavaan}
o_mi <- reliability_omega(data = items,
                          denominator = "model_implied",
                          ci_method = "none")
o_ob <- reliability_omega(data = items, ci_method = "none")
c(model_implied = o_mi$value[o_mi$term == "estimate"],
  observed      = o_ob$value[o_ob$term == "estimate"])
```

Now contaminate the scale with a nuisance doublet, a shared specific
factor on the first two items, the sort of minor structure real item
sets carry:

```{r denom-doublet, eval = has_lavaan}
doublet <- rnorm(N)
items_d <- items
items_d[, 1] <- items[, 1] + 0.6 * doublet
items_d[, 2] <- items[, 2] + 0.6 * doublet

o_mi_d <- reliability_omega(data = items_d,
                            denominator = "model_implied",
                            ci_method = "none")
o_ob_d <- reliability_omega(data = items_d, ci_method = "none")
c(model_implied = o_mi_d$value[o_mi_d$term == "estimate"],
  observed      = o_ob_d$value[o_ob_d$term == "estimate"])
```

The two now diverge, and only robust omega still
answers the question a reliability coefficient is supposed to answer,
the proportion of the variance of the composite you actually computed
that is attributable to the common factor. The divergence itself is
diagnostic: a nontrivial gap between the two denominators says the
one-factor model is not reproducing the composite variance, and the
right response to a large gap is to model the structure, not to
choose a denominator. Note also what the observed denominator does
not fix: the fitted loadings absorb part of the doublet under either
setting, so checking the one-factor model (for example with
`cfa_1()`) remains part of reporting any omega.

A small gap is not evidence of the reverse. A doublet or a method
factor shared by a few same-signed items inflates their loadings while
leaving the two denominators close, so the gap cannot certify the
numerator. The residual correlations of the single-factor fit are the
more direct check, and every omega carries them: the printed table
names the denominator and the largest residual correlation, and the
full matrix is in the `residual_cor` attribute.

```{r denom-residual, eval = has_lavaan}
o_ob_d
attr(o_ob_d, "residual_cor")$items
```

Here the largest residual sits on the doublet pair. The residuals
detect local dependence more reliably than they locate it: when the
shared variance sits on the items that dominate the factor, the single
factor absorbs it and the misfit appears among the other items.

## Categorical Omega for Ordered Items

When items are ordered categories (Likert responses, symptom counts),
the composite people score is a sum of those observed categories, and
the category thresholds make the item-factor relationship nonlinear.
Categorical omega (Green & Yang, 2009) fits the probit threshold
model and returns the reliability of the categorical sum score on its
own metric. The threshold configuration decides how much this matters
relative to treating the items as continuous. First the
benign case, every item cut at the same thresholds:

```{r categorical-benign, eval = has_lavaan}
items_same <- apply(items, 2, function(x)
  as.integer(cut(x, breaks = c(-Inf, -1, -0.2, 0.6, Inf))))
colnames(items_same) <- colnames(items)

oc_same <- reliability_omega_categorical(data = items_same, ci_method = "none")
o_same  <- reliability_omega(data = items_same, ci_method = "none")
c(categorical_omega     = oc_same$value[oc_same$term == "estimate"],
  treated_as_continuous = o_same$value[o_same$term == "estimate"])
```

With similar threshold patterns across items the two approaches
nearly agree, which is precisely the condition under which Kelley and
Pornprasertmanit (2016, Study 3) found the continuous treatment held
its interval coverage. In real item sets the threshold patterns typically differ across
items: some items skew one way, some the other. Cutting the same
underlying responses with threshold patterns that differ across
items:

```{r categorical-varied, eval = has_lavaan}
breaks_hi <- c(-Inf,  0.5,  1.2,  1.9, Inf)
breaks_lo <- c(-Inf, -1.9, -1.2, -0.5, Inf)
breaks_by_item <- list(breaks_hi, breaks_hi, breaks_hi,
                       breaks_lo, breaks_lo, breaks_lo)
items_cat <- sapply(seq_len(6), function(j)
  as.integer(cut(items[, j], breaks = breaks_by_item[[j]])))
colnames(items_cat) <- colnames(items)

oc <- reliability_omega_categorical(data = items_cat, ci_method = "none")
o_as_cont <- reliability_omega(data = items_cat, ci_method = "none")
c(categorical_omega     = oc$value[oc$term == "estimate"],
  treated_as_continuous = o_as_cont$value[o_as_cont$term == "estimate"])
```

The two now diverge, here by about
`r if (has_lavaan) round(abs(oc$value[oc$term == "estimate"] - o_as_cont$value[o_as_cont$term == "estimate"]), 2) else "0.04"`,
with the continuous treatment understating on this configuration. The
direction and size of the continuous treatment's error depend on the
threshold configuration, which is exactly what makes it untrustworthy
as a general practice; in Study 3 its interval coverage failed as
threshold patterns diverged across items. Categorical omega is
constructed on the metric of the summed categories themselves, so it
is the coefficient to report for ordinal items, which is why
`reliability()` routes integer-coded, few-category items to
`reliability_omega_categorical()` automatically.

Categorical omega and robust omega share a design principle in
Kelley and Pornprasertmanit (2016): in both, the total variance in
the denominator is not taken from the fitted factor model. Robust
omega estimates it from the sample covariances; categorical omega, as
computed there and by default here, assembles it from the
unrestricted polychoric correlations and the thresholds. In each case
the fitted single factor supplies only the numerator, so the
coefficient stays anchored to the composite that is actually scored
even when the factor model is an approximation.

Green and Yang (2009) defined their coefficient with the other
denominator. Their appendix program takes "the reproduced
(model-based) correlation matrix" as the input to the denominator, so
the original categorical omega is the model implied version, and
correlated errors enter its total variance only when they are
specified in the model. `denominator = "model_implied"` reproduces it:

```{r categorical-denominators, eval = has_lavaan}
oc_mi <- reliability_omega_categorical(data = items_cat,
                                       ci_method = "none",
                                       denominator = "model_implied")
c(unrestricted  = oc$value[oc$term == "estimate"],
  model_implied = oc_mi$value[oc_mi$term == "estimate"])
```

With a single factor generating these items the two nearly agree, as
the continuous pair did on the running example; they part ways as the
model misfits, and the residual polychoric correlations in the
`residual_cor` attribute show where.

The unrestricted denominator asks more of the data than the model
implied one. Every unrestricted polychoric correlation must be
estimable, and when two items are skewed in opposite directions their
bivariate table can have empty cells that leave the correlation
without a finite standard error: every respondent below the top
category of one item sits in the bottom category of the other. In
Green and Yang's own mixed-skew design (two items piled into the top
category, two into the bottom), most samples of 500 have such a pair.
The default then stops with an error naming the pair rather than
filling the correlation with an arbitrary value, and
`denominator = "model_implied"` remains available, since the fitted
model gives that correlation no weight and supplies its own value for
it. It does so only while the correlations the data do determine
identify the loadings, which takes a link from every item to the
others, directly or through other items, and at least one cycle of an
odd number of items (three items whose three correlations are all
determined, for example). When every
correlation between the two top-piled items and the two bottom-piled
ones is undetermined, the determined ones fix only the products of the
loadings within each pair: the relative sign of the pairs is free, and
the fit can return loadings of opposite signs and a categorical omega
near 0.05 against a population value of 0.716. Both denominators then
stop with an error naming the pairs and the unlinked blocks of items.
A bootstrap of the default meets the problem more often still: the
few respondents in the sparse cells are what determine the
correlation, and a resample often leaves some of them out, so in
Green and Yang's design at a sample size of 1,000 about half the
replications of a bootstrap of the default are set aside even when the
sample itself determines every correlation. The printed bootstrap line
and a warning count them, and the model implied denominator keeps all
but those whose remaining correlations leave the loadings unidentified
or whose solution is improper: 1 of 200 in a bootstrap of the help
page's complete data, of 1,000 respondents, but a median of 42% in
samples of 300 from the same design. When fewer than half of the
replications return a value, no interval is reported, since the rest
would be a minority of the resamples, chosen by whether they returned
one; a larger sample is the remedy. The help page of
`reliability_omega_categorical()` works through that design.

## Coefficient H: A Different Composite

Coefficient H (Hancock & Mueller, 2001) makes the same congeneric
claim as omega but changes the composite: it is the reliability of
the *optimally weighted* combination of the items, the best possible
composite the items can form, rather than the unit-weighted sum. The
two coincide when loadings are equal and diverge as loadings spread,
because optimal weights exploit the stronger items:

```{r coefficient-H, eval = has_lavaan}
res_H <- reliability_H(data = items)
res_H
c(omega = res_omega$value[res_omega$term == "estimate"],
  H     = res_H$value[res_H$term == "estimate"])
```

From raw data, `reliability_H()` fits the single-factor model exactly
as `reliability_omega()` does, and its interval is a Wald interval on
the logit scale whose standard error comes from the delta method over
the full covariance matrix of that fit's parameter estimates, robust
maximum likelihood by default, as for omega. When the factor belongs
to a larger confirmatory or structural model, pass the fitted model
instead, `reliability_H(fit, factor = "...")`, and the standard error
is the one that model was fitted with. The form
`reliability_H(loadings = , se_loadings = )` is not used here. It
builds the interval from the marginal standard errors of the
standardized loadings, which leave out the covariances among the
loading estimates; those estimates are correlated in any fitted factor
model, so its interval can be too narrow or too wide, and every such
call gives a warning saying so.

Report omega when people will score the scale by summing; report H
when scores will come from the factor model itself (or to see the
ceiling the item set could reach with optimal weighting). Reporting
both, with one sentence on which composite each describes, is often
the most informative choice under unequal loadings. Read H as a
ceiling, too, in the statistical sense: the sample H overestimates the
population coefficient, and a composite formed with weights estimated
from the sample is less reliable than H says, both markedly in samples
of about 100 and barely beyond about 1,000 (Aguirre-Urreta, Rönkkö, &
McIntosh, 2019). A bootstrap interval for H (`ci_method = "bca"`, as
for omega) also estimates that bias. Reliability coefficients matter
because researchers score and use composites; an analysis conducted
entirely within the SEM framework, relating latent variables directly,
has less need of them.

KR-20 completes the family as the binary-item special case of alpha,
provided for the classical test theory literature that names it
separately; `reliability_kr20()` and `reliability_alpha()` agree on
0/1 data.

## Choosing the Interval

Choosing the interval is an empirical question, and it is the
subject of Kelley and Pornprasertmanit (2016): across coefficients, sample
sizes, loading patterns, distributions, and (for categorical items)
threshold patterns, which interval procedures cover at their nominal
rate. The DMAR defaults are their recommendations, with one refinement
for omega described below the table:

| Coefficient | Default behavior | Recommended interval and basis |
|---|---|---|
| alpha | Bonett (2002) interval | well behaved among the closed forms under normality |
| omega, model implied | Wald interval with the robust ML standard error, its limits formed on the logit scale (`"mlr_logistic"`) | the best performing closed form when item distributions deviate from normality; the limits are formed on the logit scale on the evidence of a later study |
| robust omega | point estimate; a message names the bootstrap call | percentile or BCa bootstrap, the pairing recommended when unidimensionality is approximate; no closed form exists for the observed denominator |
| categorical omega | point estimate; a message names the bootstrap call | BCa bootstrap, which held coverage across threshold patterns, category counts, and sample sizes |

The logit form for omega comes from a Monte Carlo study run for DMAR
(in the package's source repository,
`methodology/reliability_limits`), which compared the two forms of the
Wald limits from the same standard error: the normal theory maximum
likelihood one for normal data and the Huber-White one of MLR for
nonnormal data. With normal data the limits on the scale of omega
missed mostly on one side, the population value falling below the
lower limit, and covered from .910 to .952; formed on the logit scale
and transformed back (Browne, 1982; Kelley, 2007), they covered from
.939 to .956 in every cell. With complete data the default takes
lavaan's `robust.sem` standard error, which no cell used; it estimates
the same asymptotic variance as the Huber-White one.
`fa_confirmatory()` forms the limits of its omega row on the logit
scale too. `ci_method = "mlr"` forms the limits on the scale of omega.
The examples below name the default, `ci_method = "mlr_logistic"`,
where they use it; leaving it out gives the same interval.

Coefficient H was not among the coefficients Kelley and
Pornprasertmanit (2016) compared. Its default is omega's: a Wald
interval on the logit scale with the robust ML standard error, the
fast choice, with the percentile or BCa bootstrap the safer one for a
reported analysis.

A bootstrap is never run unless it is requested. When one is
requested, `B = 10000` replications is the default.

Every reliability function offers its interval methods through
`ci_method` (Feldt, Fisher, Bonett, Hakstian-Whalen, ML, robust ML, ADF,
their logistic transformed variants, and the bootstrap family, as
applicable). Except for coefficient H, the default is the method with
simulation evidence behind it. Two practical notes. The bootstrap
methods need raw data, not a covariance matrix, and take a `seed`
argument for reproducibility. And a bootstrap interval on the
categorical coefficient refits an ordinal factor model per replicate,
so it is the slowest interval in the family; the default
`B = 10000` is a deliberate accuracy choice, worth the wait for a
final analysis and worth reducing while exploring.

```{r interval-demo, eval = has_lavaan}
# The default: robust omega, point estimate, with the message naming
# the bootstrap call.
reliability_omega(data = items)

# The recommended interval, requested explicitly (B reduced here to
# keep the vignette fast; the default is B = 10000).
reliability_omega(data = items, ci_method = "percentile",
                  B = 500, seed = 113)

# The model implied denominator has a closed form and reports its
# robust ML Wald interval by default, with the limits formed on the
# logit scale. The call names that default, ci_method = "mlr_logistic";
# leaving it out gives the same interval.
reliability_omega(data = items, denominator = "model_implied",
                  ci_method = "mlr_logistic")
```

## Missing Data: FIML and Auxiliary Variables

The `mlmr()` vignette makes the case at length: listwise deletion is
unbiased only when values are missing completely at random, and it is
inefficient always. The same argument applies to a reliability
analysis, so `reliability_alpha()` and `reliability_omega()` (and the
`reliability()` wrapper) take the same position `mlmr()` does. Two
arguments govern the treatment:

* `missing = c("fiml", "listwise", "two_stage")`. The default,
  `"fiml"`, keeps every case with at least one observed item, or with
  an auxiliary variable observed, and estimates by full information
  maximum likelihood, consistent and efficient under the weaker missing
  at random (MAR) assumption; `"listwise"` analyzes the complete cases
  alone. Data in which no value is missing are analyzed as they stand,
  with the same estimates FIML would give and the complete data
  intervals, so the default analysis is FIML whenever an item value
  is missing and the complete data analysis otherwise. `fa_confirmatory()`
  differs: it fits complete data by FIML too. The complete data analysis
  is used here because the closed-form intervals of alpha and omega
  (Bonett's among them) need it. Without auxiliary variables a row with
  every item missing does not count as a missing value, since listwise
  deletion and FIML both drop it. With auxiliary
  variables, a case whose items are all missing still
  informs the item moments through its auxiliaries, and setting it
  aside would make the analysis conditional on having an observed item,
  which biases the estimates when the items go missing together at
  random given the auxiliaries.
* `aux`, a character vector naming *auxiliary variables*: columns of
  `data` that are not part of the composite but are correlated with
  the items or with the reasons values are missing. They enter as
  saturated correlates (Graham, 2003): correlated freely with each
  other and with every item's residual, never loading on the factor
  and never entering the composite. The measurement model is
  undisturbed while FIML uses their information, and missingness that
  depends on the auxiliary becomes MAR once the auxiliary is
  conditioned on (Collins, Schafer, & Kam, 2001). Supplying `aux`
  implies `missing = "fiml"`.

```{r fiml-demo, eval = has_lavaan}
# Missingness on y2 that depends on an auxiliary z (MAR given z):
# listwise deletion is biased here, FIML with z is not.
z <- eta + rnorm(N, sd = 0.5)
d <- data.frame(items, z = z)
d$y2[runif(N) < plogis(-1 + 1.5 * as.numeric(scale(z)))] <- NA

# Listwise, asked for by name: the analysis drops the incomplete rows.
reliability_alpha(data = d[, paste0("y", 1:6)], missing = "listwise")

# FIML with the auxiliary: every case with an observed item or an
# observed z contributes, and z informs the estimation.
reliability_alpha(data = d, aux = "z")
```

The returned table always reports both `N` (the cases used) and
`N_complete` (the complete cases), so the cost of listwise deletion is
legible at a glance. The classical (analytic) alpha under FIML is
still the classical coefficient: the same formula, applied to the FIML
estimate of the item covariance matrix rather than the complete-case
one. Interval methods that cannot be made correct under FIML (the
complete-data closed forms, ADF, the profile likelihood) are refused
with an explanation rather than silently reverting to listwise
deletion; the delta method (`"ml"`, `"mlr"`) and bootstrap intervals
remain available. Users of MBESS may recognize the `aux` idea from
`ci.reliability()`, whose implementation no longer runs on current
semTools; DMAR implements the saturated correlates model directly.

### Interval Availability and the Reported Denominator

Bootstrap intervals require at least half of attempted replications and at
least two defined estimates; AVE also retains a minimum of 100. A point
estimate and counts remain available when an interval is withheld. These
rules describe estimability and selection among resamples, not a guarantee
of coverage. Defined improper values are retained under the stated policy
and counted separately.

For categorical omega with multiple imputation, the default is 20
completed datasets inside each bootstrap replication. The point estimate
has a separate adaptive rule that uses at least 20. More inner imputations
reduce imputation noise at additional computational cost; the default of 20
does not rest on a calibration of coverage. A fraction of missing
information estimated from a selected minority of replications is explicitly
qualified and cannot certify precision merely by increasing the point's
number of imputations.

A planning sensitivity result takes the probability of obtaining an interval
within the target width, the mean width, and the coverage over one
denominator, the simulated studies that returned an interval. A study that
returns no interval is in none of them; the output states how many studies
were attempted, how many returned an interval, and how many did not, and its
`simulation` attribute gives Monte Carlo limits for the width-target
probability.

## One Entry Point and the Broom Verbs

`reliability()` dispatches on `type` (or auto-detects: ordered
few-category items go to categorical omega, otherwise omega) and
forwards `denominator` and `ci_method` (here the default of the model
implied omega, named); every family member returns the same tidy shape
with `tidy()` and `glance()` methods:

```{r wrapper, eval = has_lavaan}
res <- reliability(data = items, type = "omega",
                   denominator = "model_implied",
                   ci_method = "mlr_logistic")
generics::tidy(res)
generics::glance(res)
```

## Planning the Study

Reliability estimation is also a design problem: a reliability
coefficient reported without a narrow interval is a weak claim.
`ss_aipe_reliability()` plans the sample size so the interval for
alpha or omega achieves a target width (with an `assurance`
probability if desired), which closes the loop between this family
and DMAR's accuracy in parameter estimation (AIPE) tradition. It plans
for the coefficient this family estimates from the raw item scores,
so the population is used as a covariance matrix (a correlation
matrix plans for standardized items). Without an assurance the size
comes from a closed form for the expected width of the interval
`type` names: the normal theory variance of alpha (van Zyl,
Neudecker, & Nel, 2000), evaluated on the population covariance
matrix, for the parallel model and for the normal theory interval of
the tau equivalent model, and the delta method variance of the model
implied coefficient, the variance behind
`reliability_omega(denominator = "model_implied", ci_method = "ml")`
and `reliability_alpha(estimator = "model_implied", ci_method = "ml")`,
for the factor analytic interval of the tau equivalent model and for
the congeneric model. With an assurance, a Monte Carlo search that
starts from that size finds the smallest one whose interval is no
wider than the target with the requested probability (Terry & Kelley,
2012); a simulated study whose fit returns no interval counts as one
whose interval is too wide.

## What Reliability Coefficients Cannot Do

Reliability is a property of a test score in a particular population,
not a property of the items in the abstract. A high alpha or omega
does *not* mean:

* the items measure the construct of interest (that is *validity*,
  not reliability);
* the scale will achieve the same reliability in a different
  population;
* averaging across many items has fixed the problem of poor
  individual item quality.

Single-administration coefficients also book item-specific systematic
variance as error, so all of the coefficients here are lower bounds
on classical test-retest reliability. Use the family for what it is:
an internal-consistency analysis conditional on a substantive theory
of what the items mean.

